    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across devices. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices within a layer.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices, keeping each expert whole.\n\nThese are frequently combined (TP within a node, EP across nodes), alongside data parallelism (DP) for request-level replication. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP during **inference** (prefill and decode), the communication collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\sigma(X W_1) W_2$\n\nThe canonical Megatron-style layout:\n\n| Component | Weight shard | Activation shard |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column-parallel (split output dim) | Full input $X$; heads partitioned across ranks |\n| $W_O$ | Row-parallel (split input dim) | Partial sums of output |\n| $W_1$ (up/gate) | Column-parallel | Full input; hidden dim partitioned |\n| $W_2$ (down) | Row-parallel | Partial sums of output |\n| LayerNorm, biases | Replicated | Replicated |\n\nColumn-parallel followed by row-parallel is deliberate: the intermediate activation (attention heads, MLP hidden states) is already partitioned, so **no communication is needed between the",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
